  "answer": "I'll start by exploring the workspace and reading the relevant files.\nThe bug is clear: `sum -= entry.amount` subtracts each entry instead of adding it. Note the test also requires negative amounts to reduce the total (`[{amount:-3}]` → `-3`), so `+=` (not `Math.abs`) is the correct fix.\nNow let me run the test suite.\nFixed and verified.\n\n## The bug\nIn `src/ledger.ts`, the accumulation loop used subtraction:\n\n```ts\nsum -= entry.amount   // wrong: every entry reduced the total\n```\n\nSo `total([{amount: 4}, {amount: 6}])` returned `-10` instead of `10`. The zero-entry case happened to pass (empty loop → `0`).\n\n## The fix\nOne-line change, `-=` → `+=`:\n\n```ts\nexport function total(entries: Entry[]): number {\n  let sum = 0\n  for (const entry of entries) {\n    sum += entry.amount\n  }\n  return sum\n}\n```\n\nOne thing worth calling out: I deliberately used `+=` rather than `Math.abs(entry.amount)`. The test asserts `total([{ amount: -3 }])` is `-3`, so negative amounts must still pull the total down — \"every entry adds to the total\" means applying the entry's signed amount, not discarding its sign. A `Math.abs` \"fix\" would make that assertion fail.\n\n## Test result\n```\nbun test v1.3.14\ntest/oracle.test.ts:\n(pass) totals add [0.14ms]\n 1 pass\n 0 fail\n 3 expect() calls\n```\nAll three assertions pass (`10`, `0`, `-3`).\n\nUntouched, as required: the `Entry` interface and `total` signature (exported shape is identical), `test/oracle.test.ts`, `package.json`, and `AGENTS.md`. No config was added and the `bun` runner was left as-is."
